  "text_sanskrit": "प्रशान्तमनसं ह्येनं योगिनं सुखमुत्तमम् । उपैति शान्तरजसं ब्रह्मभूतमकल्मषम् ॥ ६-२७ ॥",
  "itrans": "prashAntamanasaM hyenaM yoginaM sukhamuttamam . upaiti shAntarajasaM brahmabhUtamakalmaSham .. 6-27 ..",
  "translation": "Supreme bliss verily comes to this Yogi who is of perfectly tranquil mind, in whom Rajas is calmed, who firmly stands in Brahman and is free from all evil.(27.) \nV. Who.... mind: whose mind does not by itself seek after the objects of sense. Rajas is one of the primary principles of matter and is the cause of anger and other passions, and the evils arising from these. Hence, he is free from all evil, i.e., the evil passions, viz., desire, anger, &c., The particle, verily (hi), slows this view to be a well-known one in scriptures and other sacred authorities, (27.) \nA. When it is said that blissfulness results to the Yogi two questions present themselves: (1) Whether it is the result of his exertion (2) Or what description is it? In answer the Lord says (explains as follows).",
